nOK here's another thought: What if I define the models as (size, class) — 40 unique combos — and put config as a config_level axis where values' conditions are the config and... still can't hold per-size numbers.\n\nThe data is genuinely 3D. matrix_tables is the correct container per schema (\"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\"). The R137 model_level population works because that table is purely 2D. Here, since config dimension prevents clean model_level, relying on matrix_tables is JUSTIFIED. I'll add a small model_level for the one attribute that comes closest: permanent_flow_q3 varies by config, so no.\n\nDecision FINAL: \n- matrix_tables: 3 complete tables with numeric rows, descriptions incl. page and config axis.\n- model_family: family_name null, models: 15 sizes (meter size = model axis), attributes noting configurations covered.\n- characteristics.type_level: ratio constraints (q2/q1 = 1.6, q4/q3 = 1.25, min q3/q1 = 40), flow_rate_selection: \"Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\".\n- characteristics.config_level: 3 entries (one per config) — hmm, config_level is attribute × config-axis; I could do attribute \"covered_configurations\" axis... nah. Actually I CAN use config_level meaningfully: axis = \"bore type and power supply\", attributes = the five flow attributes, values = condition → value... can't (per-size). Skip config_level.\n\nHmm, wait, but I feel uneasy skipping model_level... Let me compromise with q1/q2/q3/q4/ratio per size for ONE table? No, arbitrary.\n\nAlternatively: model_level entries per attribute with values list where model = \"DN40 (reduced bore, battery/renewable)\" — i.e., config-qualified model labels without separate models[] enumeration. Then model_family.models stays 15 sizes but model_level uses qualified ids... inconsistent.\n\nLet me just include the full 67-model enumeration AND full model_level. Completeness. Let me write compactly: models entries as {\"model_id\":\"RB-BAT-